{"reference":"2 Samuel 11","book":"2 Samuel","chapter":11,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter presents David’s actions as an abuse of royal power: he summons Bathsheba, then arranges for Uriah’s death when the pregnancy threatens to expose what he has done. It should not be retold as a harmless mutual romance. The final sentence gives the narrator’s judgment: what David did displeased the LORD.","verse_refs":["2 Samuel 11:1-27"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_samuel_011_b7c7e0b4f62cfe2431f06e67"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"The chapter closes without allowing David’s marriage to Bathsheba or the birth of their son to settle the matter. The narrator explicitly declares David’s action displeasing to the LORD.","verse_refs":["2 Samuel 11:27"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_samuel_011_89f64bf73fcea654b664bb6b"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"In the Larger Story","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"This episode stands within Second Samuel’s larger movement from David’s accession and kingdom consolidation, through the ark and the dynasty promise, toward royal abuse, household violence, rebellion, grief, and the book’s closing appendix.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_samuel_011_0a0dbe96ba569f209df3f6f1"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A Related Reading Question","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Chronicles does not include episodes such as David and Bathsheba. That omission need not mean Samuel’s account is denied; the retelling selects material for its own interests in genealogy, temple, worship, and succession.","verse_refs":["2 Samuel 11:1-27"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_samuel_011_264cc2b038f90dc2a4ac1301"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}